#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046665} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407354} :activity/id #uuid "7eb720ac-5358-5a94-bfe5-d2ea9292b4f9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048257 :topic/id #uuid "8c7bb5de-2e24-552b-9fb3-2501f210a725" :topic/math-academy-id 4149} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407353 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401192 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "879a74f3-cdb2-5fc9-83e0-82f1953090a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=6u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401194} {:answer/id #uuid "b598f6a3-a6bb-5202-8ed8-ac9c42eb7279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the hyperbola $2{v}^{2}-3{u}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401195} {:answer/id #uuid "bca486aa-25ef-5c7c-9609-2ed1a4c5553a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the circle ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}=6.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401196} {:answer/id #uuid "1b3464e3-f53d-53d8-877c-0141084f3eb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabola $2v=3{u}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401197} {:answer/id #uuid "14c27fbe-cfe3-5924-a55c-3737ccd260a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=\\frac{3}{2}u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401198}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401198} :answer-field/id #uuid "753118cd-0a71-5bb3-aaee-283497ceb3e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f05de000-4a38-5b78-b8b6-ab15cb5a35af" :question/math-academy-id "q-160473" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2} \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=3{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)=u+v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =6u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =4v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}6u & 4v \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =6u\\cdot 1-4v\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6u-4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 6u-4v & =0 \\\\ v & =\\frac{3}{2}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v=\\frac{3}{2}u$ defines a line in the $uv$-plane."} :step/id #uuid "1d0addec-158f-59f3-a8b0-a1fbeca5ea06" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407355}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407354 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278312 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49fc877d-2b64-58a0-a27d-95f261c0a7b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the circle ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276137} {:answer/id #uuid "4db3508a-8d7f-5449-9c40-7069ccf6fd53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $v=\\pm 2u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276168} {:answer/id #uuid "855bca3a-ff78-5604-b854-6c63d00bbe2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $u=\\pm v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276677} {:answer/id #uuid "cb7df95b-b32d-5ca1-b866-576a47074953" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "The only critical point is $(0,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277292} {:answer/id #uuid "dbeaac23-e098-5ed2-b6ba-88b5f1f7e94f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277409}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277292} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc5c8f10-5fa6-51ed-b472-61ec07b1ffb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278065}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cb96d7dc-7f59-56fa-90d1-c2a71b2fab70" :question/math-academy-id "q-160460" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-{v}^{2} \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)=uv.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & -2v \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot u-(-2v)\\cdot v \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 2({u}^{2}+{v}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {u}^{2}+{v}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}=0$ defines the point $(0,0)$ in the $uv$-plane."} :step/id #uuid "85b72007-63eb-5d72-bd12-04d202f52103" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407353}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407355 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278321 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4969b933-9252-5d6c-99a3-2d0800a47ff5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\frac{u}{v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276133} {:answer/id #uuid "770127d5-5c20-5966-b228-70cc5f941617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276557} {:answer/id #uuid "b84e8d31-b0f2-5bc7-b250-aae11f693050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2uv}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277117} {:answer/id #uuid "c7cce8a1-986b-5e0d-9d7a-5c50318cedd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "4v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277256} {:answer/id #uuid "d9290ebb-9e9d-5d8a-8ec2-a3ee34d8fd40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "-\\frac{v}{u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277391}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277117} :answer-field/id #uuid "94528d39-40c7-5d22-bbd3-10b2d8db0c92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277993}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1077730f-94dd-5875-b9c2-642bfe60bb08" :question/math-academy-id "q-160582" :question/problem "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{xy} \\\\ \\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)={e}^{xy}$ and $v(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =y{e}^{xy} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =x{e}^{xy} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{xy} & x{e}^{xy} \\\\ -\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =y{e}^{xy}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}-x{e}^{xy}\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y{e}^{xy}}{x} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {e}^{xy}\\cdot \\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =2uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2uv}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9c3612bc-50a7-5cec-b6e2-2f8735720eb8"}] :activity/title "The Inverse Function Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407352}]}
